    "result_text": "The change is complete and all tests pass. Here's a summary of what I did:\n\n## Changes\n\n**1. `openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` response property (string `GOLD`/`SILVER`/`BRONZE`) with `membershipLevel`: an `int32`, `minimum: 1`, `maximum: 3`, documenting the start-at-1 / +email / +no-namesakes / cap-at-3 rule (level 4 reserved for tenure). Since `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec, the generated model now exposes `membershipLevel` instead of `membershipTier`.\n\n**2. New `MembershipLevelFormatter`** (replacing `MembershipTierFormatter`) \u2014 A small single-responsibility utility computing the numeric level: base 1, +1 when an email is present, +1 when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3. Household size is no longer an input (it was only relevant to the old GOLD tier).\n\n**3. `OwnerMapper`** \u2014 Updated the derived-field mapping from `membershipTier` to `membershipLevel`, delegating to the new formatter.\n\n## On the audit line\nThe spec noted \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead.\" I checked `OwnerAuditLogger.ownerCreated` and `ClinicServiceImpl.createOwner`: the create audit line records `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate` \u2014 it never recorded the tier, so there was nothing tier-related to convert there. No other code recorded the tier.\n\n## Tests\nThe full suite (`AcceptanceTest`, including the new `coreNumericLevel` checkpoint expecting `membershipLevel == 3` for an owner with an email and no namesakes) passes: **BUILD SUCCESS, 1 test, 0 failures**.",
